Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A Dutch Auction is one whereby the price is slowly lowered":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In an English auction, the price of an item is slowly bid up until only one bidder remains. In an Dutch auction, the opposite is true. The price of the item is slowly reduced until someone makes a bid.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a different type of auction that differs from our typical perceptions of a auction.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Revenue Equivalence Theorem states that any auction where the highest bidder wins will generate the same expected revenue for the seller\">The Revenue Equivalence Theorem states that any auction where the highest bidder wins will generate the same expected revenue for the seller</a><ul><li>Both these types of auctions abide by the Revenue Equivalence Theorem.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Vickrey Auctions prevents bid shading</span><ul><li>Another type of auction, and an associated charactersitic.</li></ul></li></ul>","An agent remains responsible for determined actions that express their character":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even if every action is causally determined, the agent may still be responsible for it, because what determines the action is the agent’s own nature, and the agent’s nature is what praise and blame are directed at.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the oldest compatibilist move available, and it relocates responsibility from the moment of choice to the standing character that the choice reveals.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Chrysippus holds the view in this form: one does not control the action, but one is the kind of person whose actions those are, and that is sufficient for the action to be attributable.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>The position this rejects.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive\">Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive</a><ul><li>The same conclusion reached by a different route.</li></ul></li></ul>","Conflicts arise when a rising power begins to overtake an existing hegemon":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Also known as the Thucydides Trap, when rising powers begin to overtake an established power, the two powers often come into conflict.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to explain/predict when conflicts occur.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This effect was first observed by Thucydides, and was noted in his <em>History of the Peloponnesian War</em>, as when Athens began to grow powerful and threaten the dominance of Sparta, the two nations engaged in the Peloponnesian War. Such conflicts naturally tend to be core state conflicts.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes\">History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes</a><ul><li>This is an example of one of the conclusions that Thucydides reached with his method.</li></ul></li></ul>","Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The first principle in Rawls’ two fundamental principles of justice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is fundamental to Rawls’ theory of justice.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The more precise statement: Equal person is to have an equal right to the most extensive basic liberty compatible with a similar liberty for others. Additionally, this condition has lexical priority over the second condition (i.e., fair equality of opportunity and the difference principle). This principle is derived from the general conception of justice by a rational agent.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>The second principle, likewise derived via the general conception of justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Economic growth and productivity does not have a direction relationship, that is, there is economic growth doesn’t means that there has been an increase in productivity and vice versa.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common misconception: that economic growth is just driven by productivity growth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Economic growth can occur without commensurate increases in productivity: an increase in available resources (labor/capital). At the same time, even if productivity may rise, a decrease in available resources may diminish the effect of productivity growth, or override it all together.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The effects of capital deepening are uncertain\">The effects of capital deepening are uncertain</a><ul><li>Capital deepening can also be a cause of economic growth.</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth\">A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth</a><ul><li>While this note makes a case that economic growth is not simply productivity growth, productivity growth still plays a large part in economic growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"SNAP does not cause economic growth\">SNAP does not cause economic growth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul>","Exempting employer-provided insurance from tax shifts compensation out of wages and expands insurance beyond catastrophic coverage":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because employer-purchased medical care is tax exempt while wages are not, employers pay a larger share of compensation in health benefits than workers would choose, and the resulting pool of pre-paid money turns insurance from protection against catastrophe into pre-payment for routine care.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a feature of American health care that is usually treated as a cultural given, namely that insurance covers ordinary expenses at all.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The exemption is arbitrary on its face, since no comparable exemption exists for food or housing. Removing it would let workers bargain for higher take-home pay and buy the coverage they actually want, which is Friedman’s proposal alongside restricting public programmes to catastrophic cover.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying\">Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying</a><ul><li>The mechanism this tax treatment feeds.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In the US, direct state expenditures are often &#x27;submerged&#x27; through indirect tax breaks\">In the US, direct state expenditures are often 'submerged' through indirect tax breaks</a><ul><li>The general pattern of which this exemption is an instance.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same\">Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same</a><ul><li>Why the incidence of the exemption falls on the worker regardless of who nominally receives it.</li></ul></li></ul>","Having a moral personality is sufficient for the full protections of justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A moral person with a moral personality is someone who is capable of having a conception of the good and someone who is capable of having a conception of justice. Having a moral personality is sufficient for being treated with the principles of justice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the nature of morality and why people would rationally want to be moral.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rawls states that only the people with moral personalities are the ones who ought to be subject to demands of the principles of justice. There is the specific case of someone whose naturally constituted such that their plan of life is fundamentally incompatible with the principles of justice as a result of their moral psychology, e.g., their sense of the good is built upon a desire to dominant others and engage in zero-sum competition. In this case, they would be punished by their society.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>This claim faces many obvious problems, such as the fact that whether someone has a moral personality is a natural contingency, and thereby is morally arbitrary. However, the difference principle is unable to solve it in this case. Additionally, this also means that people who are mentally impaired therefore don’t have rights accorded to them via the principle justice, and must rely on people’s humanity and kindness.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society\">The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society</a><ul><li>In this case, it does not.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The intolerant must mostly be tolerated\">The intolerant must mostly be tolerated</a><ul><li>These two ideas are actually compatible.</li></ul></li></ul>","In the US, direct state expenditures are often 'submerged' through indirect tax breaks":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While it may seem like the US federal government spends less than other, similar Western governments, much of the “spending” is hidden in tax breaks for individuals and businesses.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why it seems like the US government spends so little money compared to other world governments.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a></li></ul>","Industrial policy requires wage suppression":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Often, successful industrial policy has relied on states to implement wage suppression.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows that, while industrial policy can work, it is difficult to do since it requires extensive state intervention and the suppression of labor rights.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>We can look to India and Japan for an illustration of this. At the beginning of the 20th century, the two countries had approximately equal levels of wages and productivity. However, due to the more luddite views of Indian laborers, and the fact that there is an inelastic supply of labor in India, Japan was able to surpass in Indian in terms of (lower) levels of wages and higher productivity. This led to successful industrialization in the country.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This does not mean that wages did not grow, but that they did not grow as much as they could have.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>Many Asian countries also employed industrial policy in their rise to prominence.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations\">Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations</a><ul><li>This could also serve as a reason for success of East Asian nations.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice and the Meritocratic State":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Presents a philosophical defense of meritocracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important work that informs the philosophical nature of meritocracy as a defending point of view.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h5>Central Thesis: Justice is Realized when People Receive what They Deserve.</h5><p>Mulligan is a pluralist about morality, but a monist when it comes to justice.</p><ul><li>Justice and morality are not the same thing.</li><li>Additionally, being a pluralist about justice means that you can value different things (such as justice or efficiency) fairly, and not have an overwhelming bias towards one thing specifically.</li></ul><h5>Three Requirements of Justice</h5><ol><li>A theory of justice should suggest an economy that is reasonably efficient.<ol><li>Essentially, since there is always going to be an equity/efficiency trade-off, we should not sacrifice all efficiency in the pursuit of equity (as Rawls might suggest).</li></ol></li><li>A theory of justice should suggest morally attractive public policies.<ol><li>This is arrived through a process of reflective equilibrium.</li></ol></li></ol><p><code>I think that this point is relatively weak. While Mulligan claims that a refutation like 'our moral intuition may be fickle when it comes to tax policy' I don't believe that there is an 'intuition' in the same sense when it comes to tax policy at all. There may be an intuition that 'equal distribution is good', but an intuition of the kind 'the rich should be taxed x%' seems far stretched.</code></p><ol><li>A theory of justice must pass the Singer Test.<ol><li>The <strong>Singer Test</strong> states that moral opportunity costs of any public policy must be factored in. He quantifies this in terms of the number of lives potentially saved.</li></ol></li></ol><h5>Ideal and Non-ideal Justice</h5><p>There are essentially four challenges that non-ideal theories pose towards ideal theories, which Mulligan states the ideal theories (such as meritocracy) is able to meet.’</p><ol><li><strong>Feasibility:</strong> A theory of justice must be reachable from the actual world given available resources.</li><li><strong>Policy guidance:</strong> A theory of justice must be generate concrete and sensible policies that actually can serve as a policy guide. I.e., the policies that a theory creates must not unreasonably clash with our moral intuitions. ^policy-guidance</li><li><strong>Utopianism:</strong> A theory must not be utopian in nature, meaning that it must not demand too much from human beings.</li><li><strong>Justificatory incompleteness:</strong> A theory must be able to specify, down to the last details, its policy recommendations, and not remain in the realm of the abstract.</li></ol><h4>Empirical Evidence</h4><p>There is plenty of evidence to suggest that desert is a concept that is well-embedded in the human psyche, ever since we are born. It is even observed in many animals, suggesting a genetic origin. Likewise, we can present a rejection of Rawlsian justice here, since empirically, people rarely choose to subscribe to his theories. He then gives some reasons why we ought not to disregard intuition for desert specifically:</p><ol><li>If it is the case the philosophy simply ought to systematize concepts that are already implicit in our intuitions (as a metaphilosophical principle) then it is the case that since there are already a plethora of empirical evidences that demonstrate the existence of intuition for meritocracy, our work is done.</li><li>Given reflective equilibrium, the role of intuition remains important.</li><li>A theory that does not have the backing of intuition cannot serve as a justified guidance to justice. See <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice and the Meritocratic State\">Justice and the Meritocratic State › ^policy-guidance</a> .</li><li>It is <em>dangerous</em> to violate intuitions about desert, since when people live under a system that they consider to be unjust, there is more likely to be social turmoil, destabilizing social cooperation.</li><li>(This last point seems to be an elaboration on the earlier intuition point)</li></ol><h5>A Defense of Desert</h5><p>First, he sweeps away two of Rawls’ other concerns regarding desert.</p><ol><li><em>Desert is impracticable, since we can never truly find out who truly deserves something.</em><ol><li>But this is not true, since there are some deserts that are clearly observed (e.g., the fastest runner deserves the gold medals, and this is quantifiable).</li></ol></li></ol><p><code>Even in cases where it is not immediately observable who has merit, we can look to the market for such an determination (e.g., the best artist is the artist whose art we are willing to pay the most for).</code></p><ol><li><em>Desert is incompatible with public reason, that is, any distributive schemes must be justifiable to all reasonable citizens.</em><ol><li>The best theory that is compatible with public reason is meritocracy.</li></ol></li></ol><p>Then, he outlines Rawls’ argument in the following manner:</p><ul><li>P deserves x on the basis of y only if P deserves y.</li><li>If y originated in fortunate circumstances, then P does not deserve y.</li><li>All putative desert bases originated in fortunate circumstances.</li><li>Therefore, P does not deserve anything.</li></ul><p>There are currently two standard responses to the above arguments:</p><ol><li><strong>Zaitchik’s Moorean:</strong> The above principle proves too much, since it would mean that no one ever deserves anything at anytime, which is clearly absurd (reductio).<ol><li>If I baked a cake, I do not even deserve to have my cake because it was due to fortuitous circumstances that I am even able to bake it in the first place.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Sher:</strong> Rejects premise 1, claiming that the regression of desert stops at a certain point, such as effort.</li></ol><p>Mulligan proposes the following argument to strengthen their case: <strong>he rejects premise 4, that all putative deserts originate in fortune. Drawing on the concept of the essentiality of origin, some properties of ourselves are simply metaphysically necessary so that we are ourselves. Therefore, it is not the case that <em>all</em> desert bases originate in fortune.</strong></p><ul><li>The error with Rawls is that he believes that people have robust identities totally independent of their traits, meaning that you could totally alter a people’s traits, but still come out with the same person. </li><li>This line of thinking also illustrates an intuitive difference between establishing equal opportunity through either:<ul><li>Social policies, which mean perfectly reasonable,</li><li>And genetic modification of embryos to equalize natural abilities, which seems outrageous.</li></ul></li><li>An objection to the argument might be that since the assignment of genetics is purely arbitrary, it makes no sense to base desert off-of genetic assignment either.<ul><li>But this argument runs into the same issue that the previous one did, namely, that it fails to consider genetics as something metaphysically necessary for the person.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All philosophy reduces to metaphysics\">All philosophy reduces to metaphysics</a><ul><li>Perhaps that argument about the which Mulligan uses to refute Rawls can be seen as an example of this.</li></ul></li></ul>","Literature can dramatize metacognition by depicting characters reasoning about their own reasoning":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A work can take thought itself as its subject by staging characters who examine the structure of their own inferences, which turns the audience’s attention from what is concluded to how it is concluded.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the point at which drama becomes philosophical rather than merely dealing with philosophical themes, and it is a technique with a datable origin in English literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The gravedigger in <em>Hamlet</em> reasons explicitly about the structure of an act: “if I drown myself wittingly, it argues an act, and an act hath three branches, it is to act, to do, to perform. Argal, she drowned herself wittingly” (5.1.10-13). The joke is a demonstration of formal reasoning, and the audience is watching someone reason about reasoning.</li><li>Shakespeare does this considerably more after <em>Hamlet</em>, which is part of why the later plays read as more philosophical.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work\">An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work</a><ul><li>Another structural device by which a work can argue rather than assert.</li></ul></li></ul>","Marx is more idealistic than Hegel":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While the conception of the two philosophers is that Hegel is the idealistic one that created the framework which Marx then put into practice, a better way of looking at things is that Marx is actually the idealist, and Hegel, the realist.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the ultimate hypocrisy that is in Marx’s philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In Hegel’s analysis, the existing religious and political history is already reasonable, and the authorities that already exist are already what is rational. However, Marx looks at the existing history and argues that it is against reason. He argues for the complete transformation of the world according to his conception of reason.</p><h4>References</h4>","Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Natural virtue is one’s potential to become virtuous and it is innate, unreasoned, and incomplete. In order for natural virtue to become true virtue, prudence is needed. Virtue cannot be achieved without at least some innate inclination towards virtue, i.e., natural virtue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Highlights the importance of prudence to acting morally, since it is prudence that elevates natural virtues to true virtues.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a><ul><li>The virtue of prudence is required in order to turn natural virtues into true virtues.</li></ul></li></ul>","Once the threshold potential is reached, voltage-gated sodium ion channels open, and depolarization begins":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the dendrite receives a collection of different charges which average to around $-50mV$, voltage-gated $NA^+$ channels open, and cause a spike in the charge through a process known as depolarization.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial part of how muscles and neurons operate, as this is the beginning of a signal that is sent down the cells (in a neuron).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Positive sodium ions that spread through passive diffusion causes a signal to be transported through a neuron\">Positive sodium ions that spread through passive diffusion causes a signal to be transported through a neuron</a></li></ul>","Paternalistic decisions are to be guided by an individual’s settled preferences":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Paternalistic decisions are to be made so that they align with the settled preferences of the individual. If this is not possible, then the decisions should be made as to maximize the possible primary goods available to the person.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is what Rawls says about when paternalistic decisions must be made for another individual.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Paternalism is only justified given the subject’s failure/absence of reason and will.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a><ul><li>Primary goods are what allow for the development and carrying out of this long-term life plan, which leads to goodness and happiness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them\">The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Political culture represents the norms and values that society holds when they conduct their politics.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Political culture may explain the success of some societies compared to others, or, as according to Huntington’s thesis, it may shape the nature of international relations.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are four commonly recognized types of political culture:</p><ol><li><strong>Parochial:</strong> People barely recognize the government, and there is minimal political participation. Common in tribal societies.</li><li><strong>Subject:</strong> People see the government as powerful, but as something that they cannot influence; they simply obey the authority of the government.</li><li><strong>Participant:</strong> People are active, engaged, and believe that they can influence politics.</li><li><strong>Civic Culture:</strong> A mix of the previous; enough participation to ensure accountability, but enough passivity to ensure stability.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics\">Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political attitudes is one&#x27;s opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much\">Political attitudes is one's opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much</a></li></ul>","Shame can be natural or moral":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls identifies two types of shame: moral and natural.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the two types of shame that is possible for someone to have, and their different implications.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Moral shame arises from a failure to manifest or betrayal of some virtues that is explicated in one’s long-term rational plan of life. Natural shame comes from feeling deficient relative to one’s own ideals of excellence.</p><h4>References</h4>","Some people must deserve something":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A Moorean refutation of the idea that nobody deserves anything (as per luck egalitarianism) by claiming that, <em>surely</em>, <em>some</em> people deserve <em>some</em> things.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant argument against the claims made by luck egalitarianism and Rawls.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is specifically outlined in Zaitchik’s “On Deserving to Deserve.” The target is the regress principle \"If A deserves X in virtue of Y, then A must also deserve Y.\" He rejects it as a suppressed premise: one can deserve the outcome of one's effort without deserving the capacity to exert effort. If Zaitchik is right, that desert does not require ELC as a precondition, it requires only that the agent exercises agency in the relevant way.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>Mankiw offers a second argument to the same conclusion, from the other end. A distribution that perfectly equalised utility would reward the more and the less productive alike, and almost nobody accepts that; the residue of that refusal is a desert claim, whatever the argument against desert says.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>A response to one of the claims that Rawls makes in the book.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences\">Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences</a><ul><li>The same notion of desert as it appears in the free will debate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A desert claim requires both aboutness and fitness\">A desert claim requires both aboutness and fitness</a><ul><li>The conditions such a claim has to satisfy to be assessable.</li></ul></li></ul>","Taxation may justly be more than proportional to income":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle that people should contribute in proportion to their ability does not settle the schedule, and the same principle has been read as requiring the rich to contribute more than in proportion to their revenue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Proportionality is often treated as the neutral baseline from which progressivity is a departure, and this shows the baseline is already contested within the tradition that is usually cited against progressivity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Adam Smith holds both positions in the same text, arguing for contribution in proportion to ability and also that it is not unreasonable for the rich to contribute something more than in that proportion. He also endorsed a tax on luxury carriages. The flat-tax reading of Smith is therefore a selection from him rather than a summary of him.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The invisible hand was advanced narrowly and with caveats\">The invisible hand was advanced narrowly and with caveats</a><ul><li>The same pattern of selective citation, on a more famous point.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system\">Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system</a><ul><li>A modern route to the same schedule.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system\">The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system</a><ul><li>The institutional form the question takes now.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Romans learned from the people they conquered":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the key features of the Romans were their readiness to adapt. They would adopt the most effective means of accomplishing something, even if it meant copying from their enemies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains their success in creating such a large empire and ruling it successfully.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The Romans adopted the Greek style of cavalry which provided better equipment for them and allowed the units to be more useful in combat.</li><li>They also copied the Gladius from Spanish tribes which they fought against.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Happiness is the final goal of a human life, so one’s efforts should be directed towards achieving happiness in life. And to achieve happiness as Aristotle defines it, we must live a complete life of virtuous action (goodness), and live with comfortable material means.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the final goal of Aristotle’s ethical philosophy is to promote human happiness. This is what it attempts to achieve in his <em>Ethics</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are three crucial components to living a good life:</p><ol><li>Sufficient material conditions.</li><li>Virtuous action of the soul (being a good human), enabled by (1).</li><li>A complete life (maintaining virtuous action through the entirety of one’s life).</li></ol><p>Only with these three components, is one able to be happy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s method to achieving happiness is through long goodness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Contemplation is the highest form of happiness\">Contemplation is the highest form of happiness</a><ul><li>The greatest form of happiness, the topic of this note, is a contemplative one.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a><ul><li>Aristotle comes up with multiple different definitions for happiness, though the one he finds the most satisfying is above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness\">Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness</a><ul><li>Happiness not only requires virtue, but also comfortable living conditions. An elaboration of a concept form this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Another elaboration of a concept discussed herein.</li></ul></li></ul>","The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The expectations of some representative person is determined by the primary goods available to them. This is derived from the definition of the good as one’s achievement of one’s long-term rational plan, which requires primary goods.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This allows us to pinpoint down what someone’s “expectations” means.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>A few things to consider when constructing the index which makes the construction easier:</p><ol><li>Equal basic liberties account for equality of rights and liberties.</li><li>Income and power are typically correlated.</li><li>Only the least advantaged group needs to be indexed at any time, as per the difference principle.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a><ul><li>This is the definition referred to in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The priority problem has several solutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The priority problem can be solved with the bargaining in the original position, lexical priority, the adoption of singular principles, and viewing the problem from the prudential interests of the worst-off person.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The various solutions to the priority problem is needed, since it is a pressing concern with the development of any conception of justice.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some solutions that Rawls proposes for the priority problem include:</p><ol><li>Using the reason that a principle was derived in the original position to support giving the principle certain weights when it comes to the priority of the moral principles.</li><li>Use lexical priority, and justify the lexical ordering.</li><li>Adopt a single principle (e.g., utilitarianism, though Rawls rejects uilitarianism).</li><li>Replacing this broad ethical question with prudentially consider the moral implications of conflicting moral principles from the view of the worst-off in society, and make a decision based on that.</li></ol><p>The difference between 1 and 2 lies in the fact that when weighing, the principles can be applied to different degrees with the resolution of some particular conflict depending on how much of each value is at stake. Lexical ordering, on the other hand, demands that one “exhaust” the application of one principle before moving onto the next. Additionally, solution 4 is intended to reduce our reliance on intuitions when it comes to making moral decisions, which is a problem with the other three approaches.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns\">Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns</a><ul><li>Justification for lexical priority.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle\">Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>It solves the priority problem, or at least is a possible solution for it.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>Part of Rawls’ argument against utilitarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>During the dynastic period of China, highly influenced by Confucianism, many government officials in China were also scholars.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Their existence was a testament to the Meritocracy that was present in Chinese society at the time, though the Imperial Examination System. It also strengthened the influence of Confucianism in Chinese governmental institutions.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>The doctrinal source of the scholar-official ideal.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>The mechanism that recruited and bound this class to the state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient\">The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient</a><ul><li>What happened to the institution that sustained this class.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a></li></ul>","We should desire less":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The elimination of superfluous desire is something that Laozi repeatedly stresses.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something Laozi believes is more according to the Tao, and that too much desire overwhelms the individual and causes suffering.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>苏辙《老子解》</em></p><p>五色令人目盲，五音令人耳聋，五味令人口爽，驰骋田猎令人心发狂，难得之货令人行妨。是以圣人为腹不为目，故去彼取此。</p></blockquote><p>The idea that one should not constantly strive for more stimulation, since that is liable to corrupt us. Instead, we should just be satiated (like the belly, which gets full) at some point and stop desiring more (like the eyes, which can continue to receive stimuli indefinitely).</p><h4>References</h4>","Yi Yi":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows three generations of the Jian family as they navigate love, regret, illness, work, adolescence, and personal crises in contemporary Taipei. Framed by a wedding and a funeral, the film interweaves the father NJ’s encounter with a former love and his struggles to preserve his business, the mother Min-Min’s spiritual exhaustion, teenage Ting-Ting’s guilt and first romance, and young Yang-Yang’s attempts to understand the world through photography.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>My favorite film, and a film that touches on questions regarding the nature of human existence and the meaning of human growth and change.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, one has nothing to regret\">In life, one has nothing to regret</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments\">In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>The philosophy of the film embodies a little of what Aristotle thought.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
